---
title: "Tests of Hypotheses in Multiple Linear Regression"
subtitle: "Test single predictors and groups of predictors with partial F-tests and t-tests."
description: "Type I and Type III sums of squares, partial F-tests, the equivalence of t and F tests, and chunk tests for groups of variables, using mentally unhealthy days in BRFSS 2020."
order: 7
unit: "Linear regression"
categories: [Linear regression]
---



::: {.callout-note appearance="simple" icon="false"}
## Learning objectives

By the end of this lecture you will be able to:

- tell Type I (sequential) sums of squares from Type III (partial) sums of squares, and explain why only Type I depends on the order of the variables;
- carry out a partial F-test for one variable by comparing a full and a reduced model;
- show that the t-test for a coefficient and its Type III partial F-test are the same test ($t^2 = F$);
- test a group of variables together with a chunk (multiple partial) F-test;
- choose the right test for a question about one variable or a group of variables.
:::

## Introduction

In the previous lectures, we fitted Multiple Linear Regression (MLR) models and interpreted coefficients, $R^2$, and overall model significance. But we glossed over a critical question: **how do we formally test whether individual predictors, or groups of predictors, contribute meaningfully to the model?**

This lecture focuses on **hypothesis testing within the MLR framework**. We will cover:

- **Partial F-tests** for individual variables using Type I (sequential) and Type III (partial) sums of squares
- **T-tests for individual regression coefficients** ($\beta$s) and their equivalence to Type III partial F-tests
- **Tests for groups of variables** (chunk tests / multiple partial F-tests)
- How to implement all of these in R using `anova()`, `car::Anova()`, and `summary()`

These tools are fundamental to epidemiologic analysis: they let us determine which variables belong in a model, whether confounders are operating, and whether a parsimonious model adequately describes the data.

## Setup and Data


::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
library(tidyverse)
library(brfssdata)
library(knitr)
library(kableExtra)
library(broom)
library(gtsummary)
library(car)
```
:::


### The BRFSS 2020 Dataset

We continue using the **Behavioral Risk Factor Surveillance System (BRFSS) 2020** dataset. Our research question remains:

> *What individual, behavioral, and socioeconomic factors are associated with the number of mentally unhealthy days in the past 30 days among U.S. adults?*

This lecture uses the same 5,000 adults as [Multiple Linear Regression](multiple-linear-regression.qmd). The code below rebuilds that sample with the brfssdata package: it reads eight BRFSS 2020 variables, recodes them exactly as in that lecture, and draws the same random sample.


::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code  code-fold="true" code-summary="Show the data code (same as Multiple Linear Regression)"}
# Read the eight BRFSS 2020 variables (CDC names; names that start with an
# underscore need quotes)
brfss_full <- read_brfss(
  2020,
  vars = c("MENTHLTH", "PHYSHLTH", "SLEPTIM1", "_AGE80", "INCOME2", "SEXVAR",
           "EXERANY2", "_BMI5")
) |>
  janitor::clean_names()

brfss_mlr <- brfss_full |>
  mutate(
    # Outcome: mentally unhealthy days in past 30 (88 = none = 0; 77/99 = DK/refused = NA)
    menthlth_days = case_when(
      menthlth == 88              ~ 0,
      menthlth >= 1 & menthlth <= 30 ~ as.numeric(menthlth),
      TRUE                        ~ NA_real_
    ),
    # Physical health days (key predictor)
    physhlth_days = case_when(
      physhlth == 88              ~ 0,
      physhlth >= 1 & physhlth <= 30 ~ as.numeric(physhlth),
      TRUE                        ~ NA_real_
    ),
    # Sleep hours (practical cap at 14)
    sleep_hrs = case_when(
      sleptim1 >= 1 & sleptim1 <= 14 ~ as.numeric(sleptim1),
      TRUE                        ~ NA_real_
    ),
    # Age (capped at 80 per BRFSS coding)
    age = age80,
    # Income category (ordinal 1–8)
    income_cat = case_when(
      income2 %in% 1:8 ~ as.numeric(income2),
      TRUE             ~ NA_real_
    ),
    # Sex
    sex = factor(sexvar, levels = c(1, 2), labels = c("Male", "Female")),
    # Exercise in past 30 days (any physical activity)
    exercise = factor(case_when(
      exerany2 == 1 ~ "Yes",
      exerany2 == 2 ~ "No",
      TRUE          ~ NA_character_
    ), levels = c("No", "Yes")),
    # BMI (stored as integer × 100 in BRFSS)
    bmi = ifelse(bmi5 > 0, bmi5 / 100, NA_real_),
    # Income as labeled factor (for display)
    income_f = factor(income2, levels = 1:8,
      labels = c("<$10k", "$10-15k", "$15-20k", "$20-25k",
                 "$25-35k", "$35-50k", "$50-75k", ">$75k"))
  ) |>
  filter(
    !is.na(menthlth_days),
    !is.na(physhlth_days),
    !is.na(sleep_hrs),
    !is.na(age),   age >= 18,
    !is.na(income_cat),
    !is.na(sex),
    !is.na(exercise)
  )

# ── Analytic sample (reproducible random sample) ────────────────────────────
set.seed(553)
brfss_mlr <- brfss_mlr |>
  select(menthlth_days, physhlth_days, sleep_hrs, age,
         income_cat, income_f, sex, exercise, bmi) |>
  slice_sample(n = 5000)
```
:::



